# Trivy ignore file for the production image scan (.github/workflows/docker-scan.yml).
#
# Same philosophy as the `pnpm audit` allowlist in package.json / SECURITY.md:
# mute ONLY findings that are not reachable in the shipped runtime, each with a
# rationale and a review date. The image scan runs `--ignore-unfixed`, so
# unfixable base-OS CVEs are already excluded; this file is for FIXABLE findings
# that live in base-image tooling the runtime never invokes.
#
# The runner stage executes `node server.js` (the Next.js standalone server) as
# an unprivileged user. The base image's bundled package-manager CLIs (npm, npx,
# corepack, yarn) are DELETED from the runner stage (see Dockerfile), so their
# vendored dependencies (tar, pacote, sigstore, picomatch, brace-expansion, …)
# no longer exist in the image and no longer need muting here — the two earlier
# npm-CLI mutes (CVE-2026-33671 picomatch, CVE-2026-48815 sigstore) were dropped
# for that reason (review #26 / #114). Everything Trivy still reports is either
# the base OS layer or the app's own traced runtime dependencies, which are
# gated by `pnpm audit` as well.
#
# Format: one CVE id per line, preceded by a comment carrying the package, why
# it is unreachable at runtime, and a "Review by YYYY-MM-DD" date.
